{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central claim asserts resilience 'if and only if' a graph-entropy metric exceeds an undefined 'internal diversity coefficient,' yet it never supplies the evidence criterion the problem explicitly demands: how to distinguish truly independent failures from failures merely appearing uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. Its own countercondition (shared K8s/cloud stack) is a textbook hidden-correlation case, but the target fails to state what evidence would expose that hidden correlation pre-failure — e.g., measured propagation of incidents across operators sharing the same upstream image, time-series co-failures under load, or dependency-graph audit of shared managed substrates. Without such an evidence-specification, the biconditional is unfalsifiable: any observed lack of correlation can be re-explained post-hoc by invoking an unmeasured shared trigger, which is exactly the epistemic trap the question asks the answer to disarm.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim makes an unrestricted universal ('Decentralized networks are fundamentally more fragile... because economic pressures force small operators toward free-tier dependency convergence') that is directly falsified by a well-known alternative economic dynamic: network effects and SaaS consolidation do not uniformly force small operators onto free tiers — many niche providers subsist on differentiated, paid infrastructure precisely to avoid correlated outages. The target's own countercondition concedes high-resource operators with bespoke infrastructure can exist, which breaks the 'fundamentally' qualifier; yet the main claim is stated without scope restriction. This is a non sequitur between the asserted necessity of free-tier convergence and the empirical fact that small operators can operate on heterogeneous paid stacks under different market segments.","counterexample":["A network of small nonprofit registries independently operated on regional cooperatives' paid, heterogeneous infrastructure, where no single free-tier provider dominates, showing correlated-failure resilience without satisfying the claimed free-tier convergence condition."],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}